  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u=5\\theta -1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=5\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $\\theta \\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5\\theta -1$ is as follows:\n\n| $\\theta $ | $-\\infty $ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{12}{(5\\theta -1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{12}{5{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
